"""Language-feature exercises retain their actual functional transformation task."""
SPECS=[dict(ref='13.7',method='PopulationByContinent',params=[('countries','countries'),('continent','s')],result='i',mutation='none',
 description='Use a lambda-based filter and aggregation to return the total population of countries whose continent exactly equals the requested continent. Each input element is a provided Country object with GetName(), GetContinent() and GetPopulation(). Preserve the input sequence and its objects. No match, including an empty input, returns 0. Names do not determine membership and repeated records contribute separately.',
 constraints=['countries is a nonnull sequence of 0..1000 nonnull Country objects. Each population is an integer in 0..1000000; the total therefore fits signed 32-bit int. Country name and continent strings, and the target continent, are nonnull Unicode strings of 0..40 characters. Compare strings exactly, case-sensitively, without trimming or normalization.', 'Use the provided Country API; implement only PopulationByContinent. The exercise is the functional filter/map/reduce transformation, so use lambda expressions and the appropriate language collection/stream aggregation facilities. Do not modify or reorder input elements.', 'JSON examples encode each Country as [name,continent,population]; the callable receives actual Country objects rather than mixed-type JSON rows. Returned output is one integer.'],
 why='Filtering first states the membership rule directly. Mapping a selected Country to its population then discards irrelevant object fields; adding those populations with identity 0 computes exactly the requested total and handles an empty filtered sequence without a special sentinel. Lambda expressions express these small transformations next to the pipeline that uses them.',
 correctness='Filtering keeps each input occurrence exactly when its continent equals the target. Mapping preserves one population for every kept occurrence. The aggregation starts at 0; after any prefix of kept occurrences its sum equals their total population, because the next step adds exactly the next population. Induction gives the required sum after the final occurrence. Repeated countries are separate occurrences, and no mutation is needed. The empty aggregation identity 0 covers empty input and no matching continents.',
 walkthrough='For [[Harbor,West,120],[Ridge,East,80],[Delta,West,30]] and target West, the filter retains Harbor and Delta in that order. Mapping produces populations 120 and 30. The accumulator starts at 0, becomes 120, then 150. Ridge contributes nothing because East does not equal West. Searching for west instead returns 0 because matching is case-sensitive.',
 example=([[['Harbor','West',120],['Ridge','East',80],['Delta','West',30]],'West'],150),
 time='O(n + L) time, where n is the number of records and L is the total number of continent characters inspected by exact comparisons. With the admitted bounded string lengths this is O(n).',
 space='C#, Java, C++ and Python use O(1) auxiliary pipeline/accumulator space beyond the input. JavaScript filter materializes m matching object references, requiring O(m) temporary space; reduce itself uses O(1).',
 edges=['Empty input, no matches, zero populations and repeated country records.','Exact case/whitespace/Unicode distinctions; country names are irrelevant.','1000 records and the maximum 1000000000 total without signed overflow.'],
 related='The primary Java QuestionA/QuestionB sources establish the same country-continent population computation before/after a lambda pipeline. Other languages use their own functional facilities rather than receiving a different task.',
 languageWhy={
 'csharp':'C# Where captures the target string and Sum uses a lambda population selector. Both enumerate lazily, and the nonnegative admitted total cannot overflow int. String == compares contents exactly; reference identity of distinct but equal strings is irrelevant.',
 'java':'Java Arrays.stream is sequential here. filter uses String.equals for content equality, then mapToInt produces primitive populations and sum supplies the zero identity without an intermediate list or boxed sum array.',
 'cpp':'C++ std::accumulate receives an int zero identity and a lambda capturing the readonly target by reference. The lambda adds the population only for an exact std::string match, combining filter and aggregation in one traversal without copying Country objects.',
 'python':'Python filter and map are lazy iterators. Their lambdas test the Country getter and project the population; sum consumes them once with its default zero identity. No matching-country list is allocated.',
 'javascript':'JavaScript filter allocates a new array of matching original object references. reduce then adds populations from zero. The input array is unchanged, strict string equality is case-sensitive, and the admitted billion-sized total is exact as a Number.'})]
